    lesson: 'It should not surprise the Body that a trial lawyer\u2019s hardest-won courtroom lessons turn out to be the Proverbs with a modern accent — but walk it slowly, because these are the conversations that decide households. START WITH THE RULED SPIRIT. His first law: never react to the reaction. When the other person arrives loud, wounded, or baiting, whatever you do next decides whether the ROOT need ever gets addressed — and matching their energy forfeits it. The Word weighed that skill against armies: "He that is slow to anger is better than the mighty; and he that ruleth his spirit than he that taketh a city" (Proverbs 16:32). And for the manipulator, the gaslighter, the one who argues in bad faith: his counsel is to stay unbothered and stop trying to win — which is not modern therapy-speak but the oldest picture in the faith: "Who, when he was reviled, reviled not again; when he suffered, he threatened not; but committed himself to him that judgeth righteously" (1 Peter 2:23). You can afford composure because the record is kept in a higher court. THEN CLOSE THE WATERFALL. Overexplaining feels like defense and works like surrender — every extra sentence hands the room your authority. "In the multitude of words there wanteth not sin: but he that refraineth his lips is wise" (Proverbs 10:19); "let your communication be, Yea, yea; Nay, nay" (Matthew 5:37); "let thy words be few" (Ecclesiastes 5:2). Say it once, plainly, and let it stand. BE THERE. He calls presence the highest form of authenticity — the phone face-down is a message of honor before a word is spoken. The Lord modeled presence in a crush of people: "Who touched my clothes?" (Mark 5:30-31) — thronged by a multitude, He stopped for one. And the small moments he says land hardest — remembering a name, thanking the person everyone overlooks — are the Shepherd\u2019s signature: He "calleth his own sheep by name" (John 10:3); the cup of water does not lose its reward (Mark 9:41); "Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these... ye have done it unto me" (Matthew 25:40). WHEN SADNESS COMES, SIT DOWN. Empathy is a skill most never build: stop fixing, start weeping-with — "weep with them that weep" (Romans 12:15). Job\u2019s three friends were never wiser than the seven days they sat on the ground and "none spake a word" (Job 2:13); their trouble began when they opened their mouths. NOW THE HOUSE ITSELF. The "caged" feeling — one partner guarding scraps of freedom, treating every concession as loss — is a zero-sum lie, and the Word\u2019s cure is total: "in lowliness of mind let each esteem other better than themselves. Look not every man on his own things, but every man also on the things of others" (Philippians 2:3-4). Two people racing to honor each other cannot build a cage; under the surface behavior (the phone in the bedroom, the tone at dinner) there is always a NEED — connection, safety, being heard — and the need, not the behavior, is the conversation. THE REPAIR CYCLE: relationships do not fall to one failure; they bleed out through hundreds of missed repairs. The metric that matters is how fast you return to connection — and the Word set the deadline: "Be ye angry, and sin not: let not the sun go down upon your wrath" (Ephesians 4:26). Kingdom priority is even sharper: leave the gift AT THE ALTAR and go be reconciled first (Matthew 5:23-24) — repair outranks religion. Validate before you volley: their feeling gets received before your frustration gets introduced; validation is not surrender, it is the mechanism of repair. THE TANK: the 50/50 marriage is a myth with a ledger where a covenant should be. Radical honesty — "I have 20% today" — invites the covenant answer: "Bear ye one another\u2019s burdens, and so fulfil the law of Christ" (Galatians 6:2). Love carries the 80 without invoicing it. KIND, NOT NICE: nice is surface politeness that avoids truth and quietly banks resentment; kind cares enough to say the hard thing tenderly — "Faithful are the wounds of a friend; but the kisses of an enemy are deceitful" (Proverbs 27:6); "speaking the truth in love" (Ephesians 4:15). SLICE THE ISSUES: do not let ten old grievances arrive wearing tonight\u2019s argument\u2019s coat; take the one matter at hand, with curiosity instead of a verdict to win. And BRING YOUR REAL SELF: performed perfection builds distance; confessed weakness builds trust — "My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness" (2 Corinthians 12:9). The lawyer learned in courtrooms what the Body was told from the beginning: the ruled spirit wins the room, the fast repair keeps the house, and the kind truth — spoken few, spoken present, spoken in love — is The Way.',
