    lesson: 'Darrell spoke a profound thing into this house: "Yahweh\u2019s trauma is documented in the Word, and He wants us to understand and believe Him. So like Him I look at my childhood as a garden of Eden and derive lessons from the biblical Scriptures to relate to Yahweh and the Godhead -- find all relationships and create lessons." Two movements live in that sentence, and both are pure gold. FIRST MOVEMENT: THE GODHEAD DOCUMENTED HIS OWN GRIEF -- SO YOU WOULD BELIEVE HIM. We are so quick to imagine God as unmoved, above feeling, watching from a cold distance. The Word will not let us. The first recorded emotion of God after the fall is heartbreak: "And it repented the LORD that he had made man on the earth, and it GRIEVED HIM AT HIS HEART" (Genesis 6:6). Before that, in the garden, His first word to hiding Adam is not a threat but a grieving search: "Where art thou?" (Genesis 3:9) -- the voice of a Father whose child has run and hidden. Listen to Him agonize over a people determined to destroy themselves: "How shall I give thee up, Ephraim? how shall I deliver thee, Israel?... mine heart is turned within me, my repentings are kindled together" (Hosea 11:8). And the verse that says it plainest: "In all their affliction HE WAS AFFLICTED, and the angel of his presence saved them: in his love and in his pity he redeemed them" (Isaiah 63:9). Then the Word became flesh, and the grief we could not see became a face we could: "He is despised and rejected of men; a MAN OF SORROWS, and acquainted with grief" (Isaiah 53:3). At Lazarus\u2019 tomb, in the shortest and one of the most staggering verses in Scripture: "Jesus WEPT" (John 11:35). Over a whole city that would not come to Him: "he beheld the city, and WEPT over it" (Luke 19:41). In Gethsemane, under the weight of what was coming: "My soul is exceeding sorrowful, even unto death" (Matthew 26:38). And the Third Person is not exempt -- we are told, "grieve not the holy Spirit of God" (Ephesians 4:30), which means the Spirit CAN be grieved. Father, Son, and Holy Spirit: the whole Godhead, documented FEELING. Now hear Darrell\u2019s point, which is exactly right and exactly DR-0166 (believe first): God did not have to tell us any of this. He wrote His grief into the Book on purpose -- so that we would UNDERSTAND Him and BELIEVE Him. Not believe in a caricature of a stone-faced deity, but believe the real God, who knows from the inside what it is to be betrayed, to weep at a grave, to be afflicted in His people\u2019s affliction. You can bring your grief to a God who has grieved. SECOND MOVEMENT: HE KEEPS A RECORD OF YOUR GRIEF TOO -- AND TEACHES YOU TO READ YOUR STORY BY HIS. If God documented His own tears, hear what He does with yours: "Thou tellest my wanderings: put thou my TEARS INTO THY BOTTLE: are they not in THY BOOK?" (Psalm 56:8). Every tear -- collected, bottled, written down. Your grief is not invisible to Him and it is not wasted; it is recorded in the Book the way His own grief is. And this is where Darrell\u2019s practice comes in -- looking back on his childhood "as a garden of Eden" and deriving lessons from the Scriptures to relate his own story to the Godhead. That is not sentimentality and it is not making the Bible say what you want. It is the exact discipline Joseph modeled, and it is one of the most mature things a believer ever learns to do: reading your own real history through God\u2019s hand as revealed in His Word. Joseph had every reason to read his life as pure trauma -- betrayed by his brothers, sold, enslaved, falsely accused, imprisoned. But hear how he read it, out loud, to the very brothers who did it: "Now therefore be not grieved, nor angry with yourselves, that ye sold me hither: for GOD DID SEND ME before you to preserve life" (Genesis 45:5). And again: "as for you, ye thought evil against me; but GOD MEANT IT UNTO GOOD, to bring to pass, as it is this day, to save much people alive" (Genesis 50:20). Notice carefully what Joseph does NOT do: he does not deny the evil. "Ye thought evil against me" -- the wrong was real, he names it. But he refuses to let the evil be the whole meaning; he reads his story under God\u2019s sovereign, redeeming hand. That is the method. You take your real memory -- the garden ones AND the hard ones -- and you bring it to the documented Word, and you ask what the Scriptures answer: Where was God in this? "In all their affliction he was afflicted" (Isaiah 63:9) -- He was not absent, He was afflicted with you. What was He preserving or preparing? "God did send me before you to preserve life" (Genesis 45:5); "thou shalt REMEMBER all the way which the LORD thy God led thee these forty years in the wilderness, to humble thee, and to prove thee, to know what was in thine heart" (Deuteronomy 8:2). What comfort did I receive that I can now hand to someone else? "Blessed be God... the Father of mercies, and the God of all comfort; who comforteth us in all our tribulation, THAT WE MAY BE ABLE TO COMFORT them which are in any trouble, by the comfort wherewith we ourselves are comforted of God" (2 Corinthians 1:3-4). This is deriving a lesson from your life BY His Word -- not projecting your meaning onto Scripture, but letting Scripture read your life and tell you what God was doing in it. The psalmist made it a deliberate discipline: "I will REMEMBER the works of the LORD: surely I will remember thy wonders of old" (Psalm 77:11). And you do all of this remembering under a mercy that does not run out: "It is of the LORD\u2019s mercies that we are not consumed, because his compassions fail not. They are NEW EVERY MORNING: great is thy faithfulness" (Lamentations 3:22-23) -- which means you can revisit even the hard memories without being consumed by them, because His mercy meets you fresh each time you look. And "we know that ALL THINGS work together for good to them that love God, to them who are the called according to his purpose" (Romans 8:28) -- all things, the garden and the wilderness alike, woven toward good by the God who was in all of it. THE END OF BOTH STORIES -- His and yours. There is a day coming when every documented tear, the Godhead\u2019s and yours, is answered by His own hand: "And God shall WIPE AWAY ALL TEARS from their eyes; and there shall be no more death, neither sorrow, nor crying, neither shall there be any more pain: for the former things are passed away" (Revelation 21:4). So here is the whole lesson: the God you serve documented His own grief so you could believe He understands yours; He keeps your tears in His bottle and your wanderings in His book; He invites you -- as Joseph did, as Darrell does -- to bring your real story to His Word and let Him show you where He was and what He was doing; and He promises that the final word over every tear ever cried is His hand, wiping them all away. (One pastoral guardrail, in the spirit of tender care: this is reflection WITH the Godhead through the Word, not clinical therapy. Some wounds are deep, and bringing a trusted believer, a pastor, or a wise counselor alongside you as you read your story is not a lack of faith -- it is wisdom. The God who put us in a body of believers means for us to grieve and heal in company, not alone.)',
